-- 197 SOARING
-- @scene {"title":"Soaring","cps":0.6875,"quantize":1}
--
-- A MAJOR, 128 BPM. Deliberately the opposite of 196: 196 is G minor, 165 BPM,
-- twelve texture layers and a blip melody. This is one break that transforms,
-- and a piano that sings over it.
--
-- THE PIANO
--   Source: ~/tunepile/tabs-tidal/Europe_-_I__ll_Cry_For_You.gp3-7fede367.tidal
--   ("I'll Cry For You", Europe, 112 BPM). Its lead has a high sustained phrase
--   - b4 e5 fs5 gs5 a5 - which is the soaring register this track is built
--   around. Pitch classes in the whole part are a b e fs g gs: that is E major
--   with a b7, i.e. A major / E mixolydian, so 197 is in A.
--   The phrase is 94 tokens; padded to 96 so `slow 24` puts exactly one token on
--   each quarter note - a quarter-note piano line over a 165 BPM break, which is
--   what makes it read as "soaring" rather than busy. 24 bars = 45 s per phrase.
--   Voice: mars_synths_SK1_Piano (C4/C5/C6 samples, unused until now), n 1 = the
--   C5 sample, so b4-a5 sit within a few semitones instead of being chipmunked.
--
-- THE BREAK - AND WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
--
--   loopAt DOES NOT ALWAYS STRETCH. Verified by querying the pattern:
--     loopAt 1 $ s "p94brkA"  -> 1 event, speed=1.0, NO begin/end
--     chop 16 $ s "p94brkA"   -> 16 events, speed=Nothing (=1.0)
--   These banks are custom-loaded with loadSoundFile and carry no length
--   metadata, so loopAt/chop silently leave speed at 1.0. A 2.215 s break in a
--   1.4545 s bar then plays only the first 66% and jumps back to the top EVERY
--   BAR. That discontinuity is what "the break sounds sparse" actually was.
--   Fix by measurement, one of:
--     - use a break that IS one bar at the session tempo:
--         breaks165 = 1.4229 s vs bar at 165 BPM = 1.4545 s  -> 98% fit
--     - or set speed explicitly: speed = sampleDur / barDur
--         p94brkC = 2.1482 s / 1.4545 s = 1.523   (d2 does this, pitched up)
--   Metered proof: rms floor 0.038 before, 0.162 after.
--
--   p94brkA/B/C/D are 1-bar break loops (108.5/115/111/130 BPM), not used by 196.
--   A "major change" is a 32-bar arc, four 8-bar sections, each changing the
--   slice order AND the filter AND the gain together - so the section change is
--   audible in texture, not just in a parameter nudge:
--     bars 1-8    filtered + half the slices       (intro)
--     bars 9-16   full range, straight order       (open)
--     bars 17-24  interleaved order, chopped       (breakdown)
--     bars 25-32  stripped, quiet, delay-heavy     (drop-out)
--
-- A TRAP THAT COST TIME, RECORDED SO IT DOES NOT AGAIN
--   `chop` BEFORE `slice` silently cancels the reorder - verified by querying
--   begin values: `slice 4 "1 0 3 2" $ s "x"` gives begins 0.25 0.0 0.75 0.5,
--   but `slice 4 order $ chop 4 $ s "x"` gives 0.0 0.25 0.5 0.75 (identity).
--   And `slice n "<o1 o2>"` does NOT work as a section-varying order: a single
--   `<...>` yields ONE value per cycle, so slice collapses to one event.
--   So: slice DIRECTLY on s, and get sections from the parameter arc instead.
--
-- PHRASING - the thing that took longest to get right.
--   First attempt gave EVERY note the same articulation (release 2.2, sustain
--   0.55) which read as "OM OM OM OM": a drone, not a phrase. Phrasing is not in
--   the notes, it is in the articulation:
--     legato  slow 4 "<0.4 1.7 0.85 2.3 0.5 1.2>"   - notes last 40% to 230%
--     sustain slow 3 "<0.2 0.75 0.35 0.9 0.25>"
--     release slow 5 "<0.3 1.8 0.55 2.6 0.4 1.4>"   - 0.3 s to 2.6 s
--   plus a gain contour at slow 2, an octave lift/drop, and nudge drift.
--
-- GAIN - measure, do not guess. Multiplying two gain patterns silently peaks at
--   their product: 1.15 * 1.35 = 1.55 was audibly overdriven, and a 0.45 scaler
--   took it to underdriven (ceiling 0.66). The working setting caps at
--   1.18 * 1.05 = 1.24, which measured ceiling 0.83. Overdriven = 0.89,
--   underdriven = 0.66, so 0.83 is the target band.
--
-- DANCING TOGETHER: the piano shares the 8-bar section arc of the break (the
--   same <0.75 1.05 0.9 0.65> shape as d1's gain arc) and the same SWING of
--   0.14 as the kick and snare, instead of drifting on independent sines. That
--   is what makes it feel like one band rather than three loops.
--
-- LEVELS - measured with a passive orbit probe (a Synth reading
-- ~dirt.orbits[i].outBus and writing Amplitude to a control bus; it does not
-- disturb playback, unlike hushing to solo).
--   The first "rad" version had break gain 1.6 and lead gain 1.9-2.3, which put
--   orbit peaks at 0.76 / 0.66 and master rms 0.50-0.65 - inside the tanh knee,
--   heard as overdriven. Trimmed to break 1.0 and lead 0.75-0.9: master
--   rms 0.164-0.404, ceiling 0.70. Target band is ceiling <= 0.75.
--
-- MIXING STANDARD  written for ~masterBootGain = 5. No amp: gain only.

setcps (165/60/4)

d1 $ sometimesBy 0.14 (stutWith 3 0.03125 (|* gain 0.55)) $ every 17 rev $ every 13 (chunk 4 (fast 1.5)) $ every 7 (chunk 8 rev) $ every 11 (chunk 2 (|* speed 1.12)) $ slice 16 "0 2 1 3 4 6 5 7 8 10 9 11 12 14 13 15" $ s "breaks165" # orbit 0 # gain 1.0 # hpf 30 # lpf 18000 # dry 0.93 # room 0.14 # delay 0.12 # delaytime (3/16) # delayfeedback 0.32 # lock 1
d2 $ slice 16 "0 4 8 12 1 5 9 13 2 6 10 14 3 7 11 15" $ s "p94brkC" # speed 1.523 # orbit 1 # gain (slow 8 "<0.5 0.75 0.65 0.45>") # hpf 120 # lpf 14000 # pan 0.6 # dry 0.9 # room 0.2
d3 $ swingBy 0.14 4 $ sometimesBy 0.2 (|* speed 1.05) $ s "mars_kits_909_Clean:0" # orbit 2 # gain (slow 8 "<0.7 1.0 0.95 0.55>") # hpf 35 # lpf 3000 # dry 1
d4 $ swingBy 0.14 4 $ s "mars_kits_707_Clean:3" # orbit 3 # gain (slow 8 "<0.5 0.9 0.85 0.4>") # hpf 260 # lpf 9500 # room 0.2 # dry 0.88 # release 0.14
d5 $ swingBy 0.14 8 $ every 11 (fast 2) $ s "mars_kits_707_Clean:2(9,16)" # orbit 4 # gain (slow 8 "<0.35 0.5 0.45 0.3>") # hpf 5200 # lpf 14000 # pan 0.58 # dry 0.95
d6 $ slow 2 $ note "a1 ~ ~ ~ a1 ~ ~ ~ e2 ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ fs2 ~ ~ ~ fs2 ~ ~ ~ d2 ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~" # s "dubsub" # orbit 5 # gain 1.0 # hpf 28 # lpf 400 # waveMix 0 # subMix 1 # fenv 0 # attack 0.006 # release 1.3 # sustain 0.9 # dry 0.95 # room 0.06
d7 $ swingBy 0.14 2 $ slow 24 $ sometimesBy 0.28 (stutWith 3 0.0625 (|* gain 0.62)) $ sometimesBy 0.18 (ply 2) $ sometimesBy 0.12 (off 0.0625 (|* gain 0.45)) $ sometimesBy 0.1 (|+ note 12) $ sometimesBy 0.1 (|+ note (-12)) $ note "b4 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ fs5 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ g4 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ fs5 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ g4 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ fs5 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ g4 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ a5 ~ gs5 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ ~ ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ fs5 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ g4 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ fs5 ~ e5 ~ b4 ~ e5 ~ ~ ~" # s "mars_synths_SK1_Piano" # n 1 # orbit 6 # legato (slow 4 "<0.4 1.7 0.85 2.3 0.5 1.2>") # gain ((range 0.85 1.18 $ slow 37 sine) * (slow 8 "<0.75 1.05 0.9 0.65>")) # sustain (slow 3 "<0.2 0.75 0.35 0.9 0.25>") # release (slow 5 "<0.3 1.8 0.55 2.6 0.4 1.4>") # hpf 120 # lpf (slow 8 "<4200 12000 8500 3400>") # attack 0.008 # room (slow 8 "<0.75 0.55 0.68 0.8>") # size 0.95 # dry 0.55 # delay (slow 8 "<0.4 0.12 0.26 0.5>") # delaytime (3/16) # delayfeedback 0.32 # nudge (slow 7 $ range 0 0.018 sine) # lock 1
-- d8 AIR (wind): OFF. It never made audible sound in practice (orbit 7
-- measured 0.000 when it was running) and the established bet was that the
-- track does not need it. Left here for reference.
-- d8 $ every 19 (# lpf (slow 16 $ range 1200 7000 sine)) $ degradeBy (range 0.4 0.8 $ slow 29 sine) $ s "wind:4" # orbit 7 # gain (range 0.05 0.16 $ slow 31 sine) # hpf 500 # lpf 4000 # pan (range 0.25 0.75 $ slow 53 sine) # room 0.65 # size 0.95 # dry 0.45

-- CHORDS THAT ACTUALLY MOVE (replaces the gtr chord sample).
--   `gtr` is one C-major chord, so it could only ever play one chord; the note
--   transposition pinned the whole track to A and it sounded rough besides.
--   mars_synths_DrSample_Rhodes_Chords ships NAMED chords, which is the fix:
--     index 0 = Abmaj7, 1 = Am, 2 = Bbm7, 3 = Csus4
--   A major triad cannot transpose to a minor chord, which is why two samples
--   are needed for a I-V-vi-IV:
--     n 0, speed 1.0595 = +1 semitone -> Amaj7    (I)
--     n 0, speed 1.5874 = +8          -> Emaj7    (V)
--     n 0, speed 1.4142 = +6          -> Dmaj7    (IV)
--     n 1, speed 1.6818 = +9          -> F#m      (vi)
--   So n and speed are both 4-value patterns and must stay aligned - both are
--   <...> at one value per cycle, so they are.


-- PLUCK LAYER, interlaced with the chord stabs: different euclid (5,8,0 vs
-- 3,8,1), same 0.14 swing. Carries the same I-V-IV-vi movement as the Rhodes
-- (a4 e5 d5 fs5) so the two agree harmonically instead of one being pinned.
-- cp4 is a C4 pluck, so `note` transposes it via speed as usual.
d10 $ swingBy 0.14 4 $ sometimesBy 0.25 (degradeBy 0.5) $ struct "t(5,8,0)" $ note "<a4 e5 d5 fs5>" # s "cp4" # orbit 9 # gain 0.55 # hpf 600 # lpf 10000 # attack 0.002 # release 0.2 # room 0.3 # size 0.8 # dry 0.85 # delay 0.18 # delaytime (3/16) # delayfeedback 0.3 # lock 1
